check_plan_claims.py  seed = 20261126  float tolerance = 1e-12

== N1  per-sample test risk  R_hat = (1/m) sum_c (1/k_c) sum_j L(q(c), y_cj)  is unbiased
PASS  N1a  E[R_hat] = true risk, Brier, exactly (k_c in 1..3, per-prompt laws, mixed iid/dependent ratings)  -- 240 instances, 130 with some k_c = 1
PASS  N1b  E[R_hat] = true risk, RPS, exactly  -- same instances
PASS  N1c  E[R_hat] = true risk for an ARBITRARY (non-proper) loss table, exactly (properness is not needed for unbiasedness)  -- same instances
PASS  N1d  E[R_hat] identical for iid and dependent ratings with the same marginals (independence is not needed)
PASS  N1e  E[R_hat] = true risk, cross-entropy (float, tolerance 1e-10)  -- max abs error 2e-16
PASS  N1f  variance of R_hat depends on the dependence between ratings even though the mean does not (iid var < identical-copies var)  -- mean 5/8 both; var iid 1/8 < var copies 1/4
PASS  N1g  [claimed-false case] two raters, two more only if they disagree: E[item mean] = 129/500 != theta = 3/10 (adaptive k_c biases the estimator)  -- E = 129/500 = 0.258 vs theta = 0.300
PASS  N1h  [claimed-false case] 'second rater only if the first rated 1' gives bias theta(1-theta)(L0-L1)/2, nonzero whenever L0 != L1  -- 60 random (theta, q)
PASS  N1i  k_c drawn at random INDEPENDENTLY of the ratings keeps E[R_hat] = true risk  -- 40 random (p, q)

== N2  plug-in D_B(p_hat || q) bias = (1/k)(1 - ||p||^2);  exact ERM rate E||p_hat_N - p||^2 = (1/N)(1 - ||p||^2)
PASS  N2a  E||p_hat_k - q||^2 = ||p - q||^2 + (1-||p||^2)/k exactly (multinomial enumeration)  -- 70 random (K, k, p, q), K<=4, k<=6
PASS  N2b  E||p_hat_N - p||^2 = (1 - ||p||^2)/N exactly  -- same instances
PASS  N2c  [claimed-false case] plug-in D_B is NOT unbiased: bias > 0 whenever p is not a point mass
PASS  N2d  bias is exactly 0 when p is a point mass (then 1 - ||p||^2 = 0)
PASS  N2e  with k_c varying across prompts the bias is the average of (1-||p_c||^2)/k_c  -- bias 13/80
PASS  N2f  ERM rate for N in {10,100,1e3,1e4,1e5} (binary, float, summing the binomial pmf; relative tolerance 1e-9)  -- worst relative error 1e-10

== N3  law of total variance; Corr(X_p*, Y) = Var(mu)/Var(Y) in (0,1); collapsed X_delta has Corr = sqrt of it
PASS  N3a  Var(Y) = E[Var(Y|C)] + Var(mu_Y(C)) (finite joint law, exact)  -- 150 random models
PASS  N3b  X ~ p*(.|c) independent of Y given c:  Cov(X,Y) = Var(mu_Y(C))  and  Var(X) = Var(Y)
PASS  N3c  Corr(X_p*, Y) = Var(mu)/Var(Y) strictly inside (0,1) when E[Var(Y|C)] > 0 and Var(mu) > 0  -- 149 models with both variances > 0
PASS  N3d  collapsed X_delta = a mu(C) + b (a > 0):  Corr(X_delta,Y)^2 = Corr(X_p*,Y), Corr > 0  =>  Corr(X_delta,Y) = sqrt(Corr(X_p*,Y))
PASS  N3e  sqrt(rho) > rho strictly in every such model (squares compared exactly: r > r^2)
PASS  N3f  [claimed-false case] if Y is a function of c (E[Var(Y|C)] = 0) there is NO strict gain: r = 1 and the collapsed correlation is also 1  -- Corr^2 sampled = 1, collapsed = 1; 0 random models also met
PASS  N3g  [claimed-false case] the theorem needs a > 0: with a < 0 the collapsed correlation is negative (sign flip)  -- Cov = -1/16
PASS  N3h  [claimed-false case] a = 0 (constant judge): Var(X_delta) = 0, so Pearson is undefined (0/0), not 'sqrt(rho)'
PASS  N3i  [claimed-false case] without X independent of Y given c, a judge with the right marginal can have Corr = 1 (X = Y), so the hypothesis is essential

== N3j  (not in the plan) collapsing ANY sampled judge to its own conditional mean raises |Pearson| by 1/sqrt(Var(mu_q)/Var(X)); greedy MODE collapse carries no such guarantee
PASS  N3j-a  Corr(mean readout, Y)^2 / Corr(sampled readout, Y)^2 = Var(X)/Var(mu_q) >= 1 for an arbitrary judge q (exact)  -- 120 random (p*, q); 115 strict
PASS  N3j-b  [claimed-false case] 'greedy decoding always inflates Pearson over sampling' is false: here the mode (2,1) is anti-ordered to the mean (1.1,1.4)  -- sampled judge Cov = 9/400 > 0; greedy-mode judge Cov = -3/40 < 0

