PASS  N4a  Corr(X_p*, Y) = 1/2 and Corr(X_delta, Y)^2 = 1/2 (so Corr(X_delta, Y) = sqrt(1/2) = 0.7071)  -- 0.5000 and 0.7071
PASS  N4b  Brier of the true forecast 1/2 vs 2 for the collapsed forecast: ratio 4  -- 1/2 vs 2
PASS  N4c  RPS of the true forecast 1/2 vs 1 for the collapsed forecast: ratio 2  -- 1/2 vs 1
PASS  N4d  cross-entropy: true forecast ln 2 = 0.6931, collapsed forecast +inf (zero probability on every realised rating)  -- 0.6931 vs +inf

== N5  binary example p* in {0.1,0.5,0.9}, q2 = 0.01 p* + 0.90: exact Pearson tie 0.6532, Brier 0.1433 vs 0.4119 (2.87x)
PASS  N5a  mean-readout Pearson is identical for q1 and q2 (affine, a = 0.01 > 0): Corr^2 = 32/75, Corr = 0.6532  -- sqrt(32/75) = 0.6532
PASS  N5b  [claimed-false case] the tie is specific to the MEAN (probability-as-score) readout: for a SAMPLED 0/1 judge X ~ Bernoulli(q) the Pearson values are NOT tied  -- sampled Corr^2: q1 = 1024/5625 (0.1820), q2 = 1024/19344375 (0.000053)
PASS  N5c  binary Brier E (q - Y)^2: 43/300 = 0.1433 for q1 and 1235707/3000000 = 0.4119 for q2; ratio 2.87  -- 0.1433, 0.4119, ratio 2.8737
PASS  N5d  [imprecise in the plan] the plan's Brier ||q - e_Y||^2 has two components, so for binary data it is 2 x E(q-Y)^2: 43/150 = 0.2867 and 1235707/1500000 = 0.8238 (ratio unchanged); 0.1433 and 0.4119 use the one-component convention  -- 0.2867 and 0.8238
INFO  cross-entropy (nats, finite for both): q1 0.4478, q2 1.2147
PASS  N5e  [claimed-false case] scalar L2 on SAMPLED draws is improper: the 0/1 point-mass judge (7/30 = 0.2333) beats the true forecast q = p* (43/150 = 0.2867)  -- 0.2333 < 0.2867

